-- dispatch: the bug A.11 and A.46 exist to stop.
--
-- A structural derived function is keyed on its SHAPE — the arity, or the
-- field names — and on nothing else, so both tuples below are one `t2` and
-- both records one `r$x$y`. What tells them apart is the evidence each USE
-- passes, which is why the `part` lines hang under the `site` and not under
-- the `derived`.
--
-- Bake the first requester's elements into the function instead and
-- `orderStrings` reuses `num_compare` — JavaScript `<` on strings, which is
-- UTF-16 code-unit order and disagrees with `String.compare` on astral
-- characters, exactly what A.26 refuses — while `nested` compares its inner
-- tuple with `===`.
pub orderInts : ( Int, Int ), ( Int, Int ) -> Bool
orderInts a b = a < b


pub orderStrings : ( String, String ), ( String, String ) -> Bool
orderStrings a b = a < b


pub sameInts : { x : Int, y : Int }, { x : Int, y : Int } -> Bool
sameInts a b = a == b


pub nested : { x : Int, y : ( Char, Char ) }, { x : Int, y : ( Char, Char ) } -> Bool
nested a b = a == b
